Attribution notes

No third-party runtime dependency is bundled. CRADLE.md states that the tree is
stdlib-only and that this is reported as a printed PASS by `--doctor` rather
than left as an omission; there is no requirements.txt, pyproject.toml or
setup.py, by design.

CRADLE orchestrates four parts that live in a sibling project, named in
CRADLE.md and NOT redistributed here: Project_FLAMING_SWORD, providing
`tools/sword.py` (FLAMING SWORD), `tools/defender.py` (defender),
`tools/sentinel.py` (THE REAPER) and `tools/memory_bridge.py` (THE GRIMOIRE).
Project_FLAMING_SWORD is licensed under the same CDDL-1.1 ruling (commit b49dfc2fef0178d1984437de577bfa00de8a4c32) and its own NOTICE
governs those files. `--doctor` exists precisely so a missing sibling is
reported rather than assumed.

The local model runtimes CRADLE can point at (Ollama, llama.cpp, LM Studio,
vLLM) are external services. CRADLE bundles none of them, holds no weights,
requires no API key, and is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or acting on
behalf of any of them. The table in CRADLE.md lists default local endpoints as
public information.

`--serve` binds `127.0.0.1` and has no authentication. `--host 0.0.0.0` would
publish it to a network; that is stated in the help text and is the operator's
decision, not a supported configuration.

Uninstall: delete `Project_CRADLE/` and `Project_FLAMING_SWORD/`, and remove
`.flaming_sword/` from whichever directory was served. There is no service, no
registry entry, no `site-packages` copy and nothing outside those paths.
